  <h2 id="skill-stack">The high-value skill stack for any biology lane</h2>

  <p>
    Use one core skill, one multiplier skill and visible proof. The core is the technical or licensed skill of your lane. The multiplier makes you worth more than a peer with the same degree.
  </p>

  <div class="stack-grid">
    <div class="stack-card">
      <div class="stack-card__label">Core skill</div>
      <p>Lab method, patient care, pharmacy practice, field survey, or crop and food science, depending on your lane.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="stack-card">
      <div class="stack-card__label">Multiplier skill</div>
      <p>Statistics, spreadsheets or a little Python and SQL, clear scientific writing, and documentation quality. Communication in your own language and in English also counts. You do not need to become a software engineer.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="stack-card">
      <div class="stack-card__label">Proof</div>
      <p>A literature review, a lab-method write-up, an analysis of a public health or biodiversity dataset, a patient-education handout or a field report.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    AI helps in a specific way. It speeds up literature searches, first drafts and data cleaning, so a student who uses it and checks it produces more in the same hours. It does not replace lab judgment, clinical judgment or accountability.
  </p>
  <p>
    The tasks shrinking fastest are routine documentation, coding and first-pass reporting. The safer position is the person who directs the tools and verifies the output. That pressure is real in medical coding and routine reporting, and lower in bedside care, wet-lab work and fieldwork.
  </p>
  <p>
    Stage it. Now, while you study: use AI to summarise papers you then read yourself, and keep a short log of what it got wrong.
  </p>
  <p>
    Later, as tools and workplace adoption mature: build a small workflow, such as a data-cleaning template for one type of lab result, that saves someone time and that you can show. No multiplier is guaranteed.
  </p>

  <p>
    The degree is the licence, not the whole career system. Budget separately for tools, internet, books, projects and future learning.
  </p>
  <p>
    Show one piece of work on one platform that suits you, such as a LinkedIn note, a short video or a private portfolio. Where the work is clinical or confidential, use anonymised or simulated cases. If you want help sequencing this, <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/">continuous career guidance</a> is built around a high-value skill portfolio, not a one-time session.
  </p>

